<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-tree-diagrams" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Tree Diagrams</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Calculate probabilities using tree diagrams</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Learners discover that the branching structure of a tree diagram arises naturally when they try to list the outcomes of a multi-stage experiment systematically.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Pose the activity without naming the tree diagram. After learners share their lists of outcomes, ask: <q>Who organised their outcomes as a branching picture? What would you label each branch with?</q> Let the tree structure emerge from the learners' own organisation before formalising it.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-balls-from-bag">
        <title>Balls from a Bag</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A bag contains 3 red (R) balls and 2 blue (B) balls. A ball is drawn at random and <em>not replaced</em>. A second ball is then drawn.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>List all possible colour outcomes for the two draws (for example, RR means red then red).</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>RR, RB, BR, BB.</p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What is the probability that the first ball drawn is red?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>There are 3 red balls out of 5 total, so <m>P(\text{1st is R}) = \dfrac{3}{5}</m>.</p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Suppose the first ball drawn was red. What is the probability the second ball is also red? Why does this probability differ from your answer in (b)?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                After removing one red ball, the bag now contains 2 red and 2 blue balls (4 total). So <m>P(\text{2nd is R} \mid \text{1st is R}) = \dfrac{2}{4} = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>. This differs from (b) because the first draw changes what remains in the bag.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Find the probability that both balls drawn are red.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <md>P(\text{RR}) = P(\text{1st is R}) \times P(\text{2nd is R} \mid \text{1st is R}) = \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{10}.</md>
                                This is the multiplication rule applied to a sequence of draws. The tree diagram is the natural way to organise all such calculations at once.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the tree diagram as a tool for organising multi-stage probability calculations, and to establish the two key rules: multiply along paths and add across paths.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Draw the tree on the board using the activity bag. Start from a root node, draw two branches labelled R (<m>\frac{3}{5}</m>) and B (<m>\frac{2}{5}</m>). From the R branch, draw two further branches for the second draw, labelled with the conditional probabilities. Repeat from B. Then demonstrate:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><term>Multiply along a path:</term> The probability of a specific sequence = the product of all branch probabilities along that path. For example, <m>P(\text{RR}) = \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{10}</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><term>Add across paths for an event:</term> The probability of an event = the sum of path probabilities for every path that gives that event.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Check: the four terminal probabilities must add to 1.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Point out the contrast between <em>without replacement</em> (second-level probabilities change as the bag changes) and <em>with replacement</em> (all branches at the same level have the same probabilities).
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <term>tree diagram</term> is a branching diagram in which each branch represents one possible outcome at one stage of an experiment, labelled with its probability.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Multiply along paths:</term> The probability of reaching a specific terminal (a specific sequence of outcomes) equals the product of all the branch probabilities along that path.
                        <md>P(A \text{ then } B) = P(A) \times P(B \mid A).</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Add paths for an event:</term> To find the probability of an event described by several different paths, add the path probabilities for all paths that satisfy the event.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Without replacement:</term> The conditional probabilities on later branches change at each stage because removing items changes the population.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>With replacement:</term> The branch probabilities are the same at every stage because the population is restored after each draw.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Check:</term> The probabilities of all terminal paths in a tree diagram must sum to 1. Use this to verify your tree is correct before reading off probabilities.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-tree-bag-no-replace">
        <caption>
            Tree diagram for drawing two balls without replacement from a bag of 3 red (R) and 2 blue (B) balls. Each branch is labelled with its probability; the value at each terminal is the path probability.
        </caption>
        <image width="78%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
                    % Root dot
                    \fill (0,0) circle (0.07);
                    % L1 nodes
                    \node[font=\small] (R1) at (2.5,1.5) {R};
                    \node[font=\small] (B1) at (2.5,-1.5) {B};
                    % Branches from root
                    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (R1) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
                    \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (B1) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
                    % L2 nodes from R
                    \node[font=\small] (RR) at (5.5,2.5) {R};
                    \node[font=\small] (RB) at (5.5,0.5) {B};
                    \draw[thick] (R1) -- (RR) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                    \draw[thick] (R1) -- (RB) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                    % L2 nodes from B
                    \node[font=\small] (BR) at (5.5,-0.5) {R};
                    \node[font=\small] (BB) at (5.5,-2.5) {B};
                    \draw[thick] (B1) -- (BR) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{4}$};
                    \draw[thick] (B1) -- (BB) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{4}$};
                    % Terminal path probabilities
                    \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9,2.5) {$= \frac{3}{10}$};
                    \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9,0.5) {$= \frac{3}{10}$};
                    \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9,-0.5) {$= \frac{3}{10}$};
                    \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9,-2.5) {$= \frac{1}{10}$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Balls Drawn Without Replacement</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A bag contains 3 red (R) balls and 2 blue (B) balls. Two balls are drawn one at a time without replacement.
            </p>
            <p>
                Using a tree diagram, find:
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>The probability that both balls are red.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that exactly one ball is red.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that at least one ball is blue.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Set up the tree diagram. At the first draw: <m>P(R) = \frac{3}{5}</m> and <m>P(B) = \frac{2}{5}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                At the second draw, the probabilities depend on what was drawn first:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>After R: bag has 2R, 2B remaining. <m>P(R \mid \text{1st R}) = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>P(B \mid \text{1st R}) = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>After B: bag has 3R, 1B remaining. <m>P(R \mid \text{1st B}) = \frac{3}{4}</m>, <m>P(B \mid \text{1st B}) = \frac{1}{4}</m>.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <!-- Reference: fig-tree-bag-no-replace (same tree as in the activity) -->
            <p>
                The four terminal path probabilities are:
                <md>
                    P(RR) = \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{10}, \qquad
                    P(RB) = \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{10},\\
                    P(BR) = \frac{2}{5} \times \frac{3}{4} = \frac{3}{10}, \qquad
                    P(BB) = \frac{2}{5} \times \frac{1}{4} = \frac{1}{10}.
                </md>
                Check: <m>\frac{3}{10} + \frac{3}{10} + \frac{3}{10} + \frac{1}{10} = 1</m> ✓
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(\text{both red}) = P(RR) = \dfrac{3}{10}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Exactly one red means RB or BR:
                            <md>P(\text{exactly one red}) = P(RB) + P(BR) = \frac{3}{10} + \frac{3}{10} = \frac{3}{5}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            At least one blue is the complement of both red:
                            <md>P(\text{at least one blue}) = 1 - P(RR) = 1 - \frac{3}{10} = \frac{7}{10}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>A Family of Three Children</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A family has 3 children. Assume each child is equally likely to be a boy (B) or a girl (G), independently of the others.
            </p>
            <p>
                Draw a tree diagram and find:
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>The probability that all three children are girls.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that exactly two are boys.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that at least one child is a girl.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tree-three-children">
                <caption>
                    Three-level tree diagram for the genders of 3 children, each equally likely to be a boy (B) or a girl (G). Every branch is labelled <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> and every terminal path has probability <m>\frac{1}{8}</m>.
                </caption>
                <image width="80%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.88]
                            % Root dot
                            \fill (0,0) circle (0.08);
                            % L1 nodes
                            \node[font=\small] (B1) at (2,3) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (G1) at (2,-3) {G};
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (B1) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (G1) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            % L2 nodes
                            \node[font=\small] (BB) at (4,4.5) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (BG) at (4,1.5) {G};
                            \node[font=\small] (GB) at (4,-1.5) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (GG) at (4,-4.5) {G};
                            \draw[thick] (B1) -- (BB) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (B1) -- (BG) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (G1) -- (GB) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (G1) -- (GG) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            % L3 nodes
                            \node[font=\small] (BBB) at (6.5,5.3) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (BBG) at (6.5,3.8) {G};
                            \node[font=\small] (BGB) at (6.5,2.2) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (BGG) at (6.5,0.8) {G};
                            \node[font=\small] (GBB) at (6.5,-0.8) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (GBG) at (6.5,-2.2) {G};
                            \node[font=\small] (GGB) at (6.5,-3.8) {B};
                            \node[font=\small] (GGG) at (6.5,-5.3) {G};
                            \draw[thick] (BB) -- (BBB) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (BB) -- (BBG) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (BG) -- (BGB) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (BG) -- (BGG) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (GB) -- (GBB) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (GB) -- (GBG) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (GG) -- (GGB) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            \draw[thick] (GG) -- (GGG) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\scriptsize] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
                            % Terminal probabilities
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,5.3) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,3.8) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,2.2) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,0.8) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,-0.8) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,-2.2) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,-3.8) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                            \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,-5.3) {$= \frac{1}{8}$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The tree has 3 levels, with each branch labelled <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>. Every path to a terminal has probability <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{8}</m>. There are 8 terminals in total, and their probabilities sum to <m>8 \times \frac{1}{8} = 1</m> ✓.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            There is only one path giving GGG:
                            <md>P(\text{GGG}) = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{8}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The paths with exactly two boys are BBG, BGB, and GBB — three paths, each with probability <m>\frac{1}{8}</m>:
                            <md>P(\text{exactly two boys}) = 3 \times \frac{1}{8} = \frac{3}{8}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The only path with <em>no</em> girls is BBB. Using the complement:
                            <md>P(\text{at least one girl}) = 1 - P(\text{BBB}) = 1 - \frac{1}{8} = \frac{7}{8}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Weather and Lateness</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The probability that it rains on a school day in Nairobi is 0.3. If it rains, the probability that Atieno arrives late for school is 0.6. If it does not rain, the probability she is late is 0.1.
            </p>
            <p>
                Draw a tree diagram and find:
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>The probability that Atieno is late for school.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that it rained but she was not late.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tree-weather">
                <caption>Tree diagram for Atieno's lateness given weather. Branch labels are probabilities; values at right show each terminal path probability.</caption>
                <image width="82%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
                            \fill (0,0) circle (0.07);
                            \node[font=\small] (Rain) at (2.8, 1.2) {Rain};
                            \node[font=\small] (NoRain) at (2.8, -1.2) {No Rain};
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (Rain) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.3$};
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (NoRain) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.7$};
                            \node[font=\small] (RLate) at (6.2, 2.0) {Late};
                            \node[font=\small] (RNLate) at (6.2, 0.4) {Not Late};
                            \draw[thick] (Rain) -- (RLate) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.6$};
                            \draw[thick] (Rain) -- (RNLate) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.4$};
                            \node[font=\small] (NRLate) at (6.2, -0.4) {Late};
                            \node[font=\small] (NRNLate) at (6.2, -2.0) {Not Late};
                            \draw[thick] (NoRain) -- (NRLate) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.1$};
                            \draw[thick] (NoRain) -- (NRNLate) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.9$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (6.85, 2.0) {$= 0.18$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (6.85, 0.4) {$= 0.12$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (6.85, -0.4) {$= 0.07$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (6.85, -2.0) {$= 0.63$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The tree has two stages: weather (Rain / No Rain), then punctuality (Late / Not Late). The four terminal path probabilities are:
            </p>
            <table>
                <title>Terminal path probabilities</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Path</cell>
                        <cell>Calculation</cell>
                        <cell>Probability</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Rain, Late</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.3 \times 0.6</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.18</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Rain, Not Late</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.3 \times 0.4</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.12</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>No Rain, Late</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.7 \times 0.1</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.07</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>No Rain, Not Late</cell>
                        <cell><m>0.7 \times 0.9</m></cell>
                        <cell>0.63</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Total</cell>
                        <cell></cell>
                        <cell>1.00 ✓</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The event <q>Atieno is late</q> corresponds to two paths:
                            <md>P(\text{late}) = P(\text{Rain, Late}) + P(\text{No Rain, Late}) = 0.18 + 0.07 = 0.25.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>P(\text{Rain, Not Late}) = 0.12</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Football Matches — A 3-Branch Tree</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Amani's football team plays 2 matches. In each match, independently, the team wins with probability 0.5, draws with probability 0.3, and loses with probability 0.2.
            </p>
            <p>
                Draw a tree diagram and find:
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>The probability that the team wins both matches.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The probability that the team wins at least one match.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tree-football">
                <caption>Tree diagram for two football matches. W = win (0.5), D = draw (0.3), L = lose (0.2). The W–W path is highlighted. Values at right show each terminal path probability.</caption>
                <image width="82%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
                            \fill (0,0) circle (0.07);
                            \node[font=\small] (W1) at (2.5, 2.0) {W};
                            \node[font=\small] (D1) at (2.5, 0) {D};
                            \node[font=\small] (L1) at (2.5, -2.0) {L};
                            \draw[blue, very thick] (0,0) -- (W1) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small, black] {$0.5$};
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (D1) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.3$};
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (L1) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.2$};
                            \node[font=\small] (WW) at (5.5, 2.65) {W};
                            \node[font=\small] (WD) at (5.5, 2.0) {D};
                            \node[font=\small] (WL) at (5.5, 1.35) {L};
                            \draw[blue, very thick] (W1) -- (WW) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small, black] {$0.5$};
                            \draw[thick] (W1) -- (WD) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.3$};
                            \draw[thick] (W1) -- (WL) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.2$};
                            \node[font=\small] (DW) at (5.5, 0.65) {W};
                            \node[font=\small] (DD) at (5.5, 0) {D};
                            \node[font=\small] (DL) at (5.5, -0.65) {L};
                            \draw[thick] (D1) -- (DW) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.5$};
                            \draw[thick] (D1) -- (DD) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.3$};
                            \draw[thick] (D1) -- (DL) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.2$};
                            \node[font=\small] (LW) at (5.5, -1.35) {W};
                            \node[font=\small] (LD) at (5.5, -2.0) {D};
                            \node[font=\small] (LL) at (5.5, -2.65) {L};
                            \draw[thick] (L1) -- (LW) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.5$};
                            \draw[thick] (L1) -- (LD) node[midway, above, sloped, font=\small] {$0.3$};
                            \draw[thick] (L1) -- (LL) node[midway, below, sloped, font=\small] {$0.2$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, 2.65) {$= 0.25$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, 2.0) {$= 0.15$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, 1.35) {$= 0.10$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, 0.65) {$= 0.15$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, 0) {$= 0.09$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, -0.65) {$= 0.06$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, -1.35) {$= 0.10$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, -2.0) {$= 0.06$};
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (5.9, -2.65) {$= 0.04$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The tree has two levels, each with three branches (W, D, L). There are <m>3 \times 3 = 9</m> terminal paths. The branch probabilities are the same at both levels because the matches are independent.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(\text{win both}) = P(W, W) = 0.5 \times 0.5 = 0.25.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            It is easier to use the complement. The team wins <em>no</em> matches when both results are a draw or a loss:
                            <md>P(\text{no wins}) = P(\text{D or L in both matches}) = (0.3 + 0.2)^2 = 0.5^2 = 0.25.</md>
                            Therefore:
                            <md>P(\text{at least one win}) = 1 - 0.25 = 0.75.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A fair coin is tossed and a spinner with three equal sections labelled 1, 2, and 3 is spun.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Draw a tree diagram showing all possible outcomes. Use it to find:
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The probability of getting heads and an odd number.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The probability of getting tails and the number 2.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The tree has two levels: coin (H or T, each with probability <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>), then spinner (1, 2, or 3, each with probability <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>). There are 6 equally likely terminal paths.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Odd numbers on the spinner are 1 and 3, so there are 2 favourable paths out of 6:
                                <md>P(\text{H and odd}) = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{2}{3} = \frac{1}{3}.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>P(\text{T and 2}) = \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{3} = \frac{1}{6}.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A bag contains 2 black (B) balls and 3 green (G) balls. Two balls are drawn one at a time without replacement.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Draw a tree diagram and find:
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The probability that both balls are green.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The probability that exactly one ball is black.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    First draw: <m>P(B) = \frac{2}{5}</m>, <m>P(G) = \frac{3}{5}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Second draw (after B): <m>P(B) = \frac{1}{4}</m>, <m>P(G) = \frac{3}{4}</m>.
                    Second draw (after G): <m>P(B) = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>P(G) = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>P(GG) = \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{10}.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Exactly one black: paths BG and GB.
                                <md>P(\text{exactly one black}) = P(BG) + P(GB) = \frac{2}{5} \times \frac{3}{4} + \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{2}{4} = \frac{6}{20} + \frac{6}{20} = \frac{3}{5}.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The probability that Koech passes a mathematics test is <m>\frac{3}{4}</m>. He takes two tests, and the outcomes are independent.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Draw a tree diagram and find:
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The probability that he passes both tests.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The probability that he passes at least one test.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Each branch is P(pass) = <m>\frac{3}{4}</m>, P(fail) = <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>, independently.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>P(\text{pass both}) = \frac{3}{4} \times \frac{3}{4} = \frac{9}{16}.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Using the complement (fails both tests):
                                <md>P(\text{at least one pass}) = 1 - P(\text{fail both}) = 1 - \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{1}{4} = 1 - \frac{1}{16} = \frac{15}{16}.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A bag contains 4 white (W) and 2 red (R) balls. Two balls are drawn one at a time <em>with replacement</em> (the first ball is returned to the bag before the second is drawn).
                </p>
                <p>
                    Draw a tree diagram and find the probability that both balls drawn are the same colour.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Because draws are with replacement, the probabilities do not change: <m>P(W) = \frac{4}{6} = \frac{2}{3}</m> and <m>P(R) = \frac{2}{6} = \frac{1}{3}</m> at both levels.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>P(\text{same colour}) = P(WW) + P(RR) = \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^2 = \frac{4}{9} + \frac{1}{9} = \frac{5}{9}.</md>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Amina takes a multiple-choice test with 3 questions. Each question has 4 options, exactly one of which is correct. She guesses every answer at random.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Let C = correct answer and W = wrong answer. Draw a tree diagram and find the probability that Amina passes the test, where passing requires getting at least 2 questions correct.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Each branch has <m>P(C) = \frac{1}{4}</m> and <m>P(W) = \frac{3}{4}</m>, independently. The 3-level tree has 8 terminals.
                </p>
                <p>
                    To pass, Amina needs exactly 2 or exactly 3 correct answers.
                </p>
                <p>
                    There are <m>\binom{3}{2} = 3</m> paths with exactly 2 correct (CCW, CWC, WCC), each with probability <m>\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2 \times \frac{3}{4} = \frac{3}{64}</m>:
                    <md>P(\text{exactly 2 correct}) = 3 \times \frac{3}{64} = \frac{9}{64}.</md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    There is 1 path with all 3 correct:
                    <md>P(\text{all 3 correct}) = \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{64}.</md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>P(\text{passes}) = \frac{9}{64} + \frac{1}{64} = \frac{10}{64} = \frac{5}{32}.</md>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

